[38;2;124;124;124mbe a finite abelian group. Determine whether there exists a Galois extension of [0m[38;2;124;124;124mK whose Galois group is isomorphic to G.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is the inverse Galois problem for abelian groups over number fields. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124manswer is YES — for any finite abelian group G and any number field K, there [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexists a Galois extension L/K with Gal(L/K) ≅ G.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis follows from class field theory. Specifically, the Kronecker-Weber theorem [0m[38;2;124;124;124mgeneralizes... actually let me think carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key result: For any number field K and any finite abelian group G, there [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexists a finite abelian extension L/K with Gal(L/K) ≅ G.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a consequence of the existence theorem of class field theory (Takagi [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mexistence theorem). Let me recall the precise statement.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mClass field theory: For a number field K, there is a bijection between finite [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mabelian extensions L/K and congruence subgroups (modulus m, subgroup of the ray [0m[38;2;124;124;124mclass group mod m). The Galois group Gal(L/K) is isomorphic to the ray class [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgroup modulo m modulo the congruence subgroup.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe existence theorem states: For every modulus m and every subgroup H of the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mray class group C_m, there exists a unique abelian extension L/K such that the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mconductor of L divides m and the corresponding congruence subgroup is H.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo to realize a given finite abelian group G, we need to find a modulus m such [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat the ray class group C_m has a quotient isomorphic to G.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the standard approach: We need to show that for any finite abelian [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgroup G, there exists a modulus m such that G is a quotient of the ray class [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgroup C_m.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe ray class group C_m fits in the exact sequence:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1 → (O_K/m)* / (image of O_K*) → C_m → Cl(O_K) → 1[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mwhere Cl(O_K) is the class group.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key point: by choosing m to include sufficiently many prime factors with [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhigh exponents, the group (O_K/m)* can be made to have arbitrarily large [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mquotients of the form (Z/nZ)* for various n, and in particular can surject onto [0m[38;2;124;124;124many finite abelian group.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mMore precisely: By Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions (or [0m[38;2;124;124;124mmore precisely, by Chebotarev density theorem applied to appropriate [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mextensions), for any integer n, there exist infinitely many prime ideals p of [0m
